Section name: Background
Excerpt:
In this section, we summarize the key concepts and definitions used in the subsequent discussions. We assume a range space (D,R), whereDis the dataset, and Ris a collection of geometric ranges defined overD, such as all possible balls, half-spaces, or other regions in R𝑑. VC-Dimension. The Vapnik-Chervonenkis (VC) dimension [ 50] is a fundamental combinatorial measure of complexity for a range space. Formally, the VC-dimension is the size of the largest subset 𝐶⊆D such that every subset of 𝐶can be realized as the intersection of𝐶with some range in R. A low VC-dimension implies that the range space has limited expressive power, which in turn allows for efficient sampling-based approximations (see [ 27] for more details). 𝜀-Samples. An𝜀-sample (or 𝜀-approximation) of a range space (D,R)is a subsetN⊆D such that, for every range 𝑅∈R, the proportion of points in 𝑅is approximately preserved in

